s area b and white cells 2b, we need for each black cell (i,j): h_i * w_j = b, and each white cell (k,l): h_k * w_l = 2b.\n\nBut in a grid, each row height h_i is shared by all cells in that row, and each column width w_j is shared by all cells in that column. Therefore, if a row i contains both black and white cells, then for that row, h_i must be such that h_i * w_j = b for black cells and h_i * w_l = 2b for white cells. Therefore, in such a row, the widths w_j and w_l must satisfy w_l = 2w_j. Similarly, in a column that contains both black and white cells, the heights must satisfy h_k = 2h_i.\n\nBut in an 8x8 checkerboard, each row has four black and four white cells alternating. Therefore, in each row, the columns must alternate between w_j and 2w_j. Similarly, each column has four black and four white cells, so the rows must alternate between h_i and 2h_i. But this creates a problem because the widths and heights are consistent across all rows and columns.\n\nLet\u2019s attempt to construct such a grid.\n\nSuppose in each row, the columns alternate between width x and 2x. Since there are eight columns, four of each, the total width per row would be 4x + 4*(2x) = 12x. But the total width must be 1, so 12x =1 \u21d2 x =1/12. Therefore, column widths are alternating 1/12 and 2/12=1/6.\n\nSimilarly, in each column, the rows alternate between height y and 2y. Each column has eight cells, four of each height, so total height per column is 4y +4*(2y) =12y=1 \u21d2 y=1/12. Therefore, row heights alternate between1/12 and 2/12=1/6.\n\nNow, let's compute the area of each cell. In a row with height h and column with width w, the area is h*w.\n\n- For a black cell in an odd row and even column: odd rows have height y=1/12, even columns have width 2x=1/6. Area= (1/12)*(1/6)=1/72.\n\n- For a black cell in an even row and odd column: even rows have height 2y=1/6, odd columns have width x=1/12. Area=(1/6)*(1/12)=1/72.\n\nTherefore, all black cells have area 1/72.\n\n- For a white cell in an odd row and odd column: height 1/12, width 1/12. Area=1/144.\n\n- For a white cell in an even row and even column: height 1/6, width 1/6. Area=1/36.\n\nBut wait, white cells have two different areas:1/144 and1/36. The ratio of white cell areas is 36/144=1/4, which is allowed because the problem only restricts white to black ratio. The white cells have areas 1/144 and1/36, while black cells are all1/72.\n\nSo check the white to black ratios:\n\n- 1/36 divided by1/72 =2, which is allowed.\n\n-1/144 divided by1/72=0.5, which is allowed.\n\nTherefore, this configuration satisfies the problem's condition. The total white area is 16*(1/36) +16*(1/144)=16/36 +16/144=4/9 +1/9=5/9. The total black area is32*(1/72)=32/72=4/9. Therefore, the ratio is5/9 divided by4/9=5/4=1.25.\n\nThis is the same ratio as before. So even if we try to set all black cells to the same area and white cells to twice that area, the grid structure forces some white cells to be smaller, leading to the same total ratio of5/4.\n\nTherefore, it seems that the maximum ratio achievable is5/4.\n\nBut wait, the problem says \"the ratio of the area of any white cell to the area of any black cell is at most 2\". In this configuration, we have white cells with area1/36 and1/144. The black cells are1/72. The ratio of white to black cells is either2 or0.5, both of which are allowed since the problem only requires white/black \u22642. So0.5 is fine.\n\nTherefore, this configuration is valid and gives a total ratio of5/4. Is there a way to make it higher?\n\nSuppose we try to have three different column widths or row heights. For example, some columns very narrow and some wide. But the problem is that with the checkerboard pattern, varying more widths and heights would create more variation in cell areas, but due to the constraint that every white cell must be \u22642*every black cell, the white cells cannot exceed twice the smallest black cell. So maybe making some black cells very small would force