{7}.", "deepseek_solution": "To find the area of a regular octagon with sides alternating between lengths 1 and \\(\\sqrt{2}\\), we can use the shoelace formula after determining the coordinates of each vertex. \n\n1. **Determine the coordinates of the vertices**:\n   - Start at \\((0, 0)\\).\n   - Move right 1 unit to \\((1, 0)\\).\n   - Turn 45\u00b0, move \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) units to \\((2, 1)\\).\n   - Turn 45\u00b0, move up 1 unit to \\((2, 2)\\).\n   - Turn 45\u00b0, move \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) units to \\((1, 3)\\).\n   - Turn 45\u00b0, move left 1 unit to \\((0, 3)\\).\n   - Turn 45\u00b0, move \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) units to \\((-1, 2)\\).\n   - Turn 45\u00b0, move down 1 unit to \\((-1, 1)\\).\n   - Turn 45\u00b0, move \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) units back to \\((0, 0)\\).\n\n2. **List the coordinates of the vertices**:\n   - \\((0, 0)\\)\n   - \\((1, 0)\\)\n   - \\((2, 1)\\)\n   - \\((2, 2)\\)\n   - \\((1, 3)\\)\n   - \\((0, 3)\\)\n   - \\((-1, 2)\\)\n   - \\((-1, 1)\\)\n\n3. **Apply the shoelace formula**:\n   - Calculate each term \\(x_i y_{i+1} - x_{i+1} y_i\\):\n     - \\(0 \\cdot 0 - 1 \\cdot 0 = 0\\)\n     - \\(1 \\cdot 1 - 2 \\cdot 0 = 1\\)\n     - \\(2 \\cdot 2 - 2 \\cdot 1 = 2\\)\n     - \\(2 \\cdot 3 - 1 \\cdot 2 = 4\\)\n     - \\(1 \\cdot 3 - 0 \\cdot 3 = 3\\)\n     - \\(0 \\cdot 2 - (-1) \\cdot 3 = 3\\)\n     - \\((-1) \\cdot 1 - (-1) \\cdot 2 = 1\\)\n     - \\((-1) \\cdot 0 - 0 \\cdot 1 = 0\\)\n   - Sum these terms: \\(0 + 1 + 2 + 4 + 3 + 3 + 1 + 0 = 14\\)\n   - The area is \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times |14| = 7\\).\n\nThus, the area of the octagon is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "1. To find the area of the equiangular octagon with alternating side lengths 1 and $\\sqrt{2}$, let's start by understanding its geometric construction. \n\n2. We will assume that the vertex angles are all equal, which means each internal angle of the octagon is $135^\\circ$, since the sum of the internal angles of an \\(n\\)-sided polygon is \\((n-2) \\times 180^\\circ\\) and for an 