e reflection of line g across the midpoint of AB.\n\nHence, the box answer is \\boxed{\\text{The reflection of line } g \\text{ across the midpoint of segment } AB}\n\nBut to adhere to mathematical problem conventions, where the answer might be phrased more geometrically. Since reflecting a line over a point (the midpoint) results in a parallel line if the original line is perpendicular to the segment. Wait, no: reflecting a vertical line over the midpoint would result in another vertical line. Since in our coordinate system, reflecting over the midpoint (a point) would reverse the distance. So, if g is at x = h, the reflection is x = 2a - h if AB is from 0 to 2a. Therefore, the line x = 2a - h is the reflection.\n\nThus, in conclusion, the locus is the line perpendicular to AB that is the reflection of g over the midpoint of AB. Therefore, the answer is \\boxed{\\text{The reflection of line } g \\text{ over the midpoint of segment } AB}\n\nThis is concise and uses geometric terminology appropriately.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the locus of the intersection points of the perpendiculars constructed at points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) as point \\( P \\) moves along line \\( g \\), we start by setting up a coordinate system. Let segment \\( AB \\) be horizontal with \\( A \\) at \\((0, 0)\\) and \\( B \\) at \\((d, 0)\\). Line \\( g \\), being perpendicular to \\( AB \\), is a vertical line. Assume \\( g \\) intersects \\( AB \\) at point \\((h, 0)\\). For any point \\( P \\) on \\( g \\) with coordinates \\((h, k)\\), we connect \\( P \\) to \\( A \\) and \\( B \\).\n\nThe slopes of lines \\( PA \\) and \\( PB \\) are \\(\\frac{k}{h}\\) and \\(\\frac{k}{h - d}\\) respectively. The perpendiculars to \\( PA \\) at \\( A \\) and to \\( PB \\) at \\( B \\) have slopes \\(-\\frac{h}{k}\\) and \\(-\\frac{h - d}{k}\\). These perpendiculars intersect at a point \\((x, y)\\).\n\nSolving the equations of the perpendiculars:\n1. Perpendicular to \\( PA \\) at \\( A \\): 