ure that the proper divisors of \\( n \\) are exactly \\( p - 1 \\), and that \\( n \\) is composite.\n\nBut in the cases we saw:\n\n- \\( m = 9 \\) (prime square): \\( n = 4 \\), which is composite, proper divisor 2 = 3 - 1.\n\n- \\( m = 15 \\) (product of two primes): \\( n = 8 \\), composite, proper divisors 2 and 4 = 3 - 1 and 5 - 1.\n\nSo, if we can find all such \\( m \\), then we can find all \\( n \\).\n\nBut how to ensure that the proper divisors of \\( n \\) are exactly \\( p - 1 \\).\n\nIn the case where \\( m \\) is a product of two distinct primes, \\( m = p*q \\), then \\( n = (p - 1)(q - 1) \\). The proper divisors of \\( n \\) would be the divisors of \\( n \\) excluding 1 and \\( n \\). For \\( n \\) to have proper divisors exactly \\( p - 1 \\) and \\( q - 1 \\), \\( (p - 1) \\) and \\( (q - 1) \\) must be the only proper divisors of \\( n \\).\n\nThis requires that \\( (p - 1) \\) and \\( (q - 1) \\) are primes themselves, or that their product \\( n \\) has no other divisors.\n\nBut wait, in our earlier example, \\( p = 3 \\), \\( q = 5 \\), \\( n = 2 * 4 = 8 \\). The proper divisors of 8 are 2 and 4. Which correspond to \\( p - 1 = 2 \\) and \\( q - 1 = 4 \\). But 4 is not prime. Wait, but in this case, \\( n = 8 \\) is a prime power, so its proper divisors are powers of 2. Hence, even though 4 is not prime, the proper divisors of \\( n \\) are 2 and 4. So, this case works because \\( n \\) is a prime power, and hence its proper divisors are also powers of that prime. Therefore, even if \\( p - 1 \\) and \\( q - 1 \\) are not primes, but when multiplied together give a prime power, then the proper divisors would be the factors of that prime power.\n\nWait, this complicates things. Let's analyze:\n\nIf \\( m \\) is a product of two distinct primes \\( p \\) and \\( q \\), then \\( n = (p - 1)(q - 1) \\). For \\( n \\) to be a prime power, \\( (p - 1) \\) and \\( (q - 1) \\) must be powers of the same prime.\n\nIn the case of \\( m = 15 \\), \\( p = 3 \\), \\( q = 5 \\). Then, \\( p - 1 = 2 \\), \\( q - 1 = 4 \\), which are both powers of 2. Hence, \\( n = 2 * 4 = 8 = 2^3 \\), a prime power. Its proper divisors are 2 and 4.\n\nSimilarly, if we take other primes \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) such that \\( p - 1 \\) and \\( q - 1 \\) are powers of the same prime, then \\( n \\) will be a prime power, and its proper divisors will be those powers.\n\nFor example, let's see if there are other such primes.\n\nTake \\( p = 5 \\), \\( q - 1 = 8 \\). So, \\( q = 9 \\), but 9 is not prime. Not valid.\n\nTake \\( p = 7 \\), \\( q - 1 = 16 \\). Then \\( q = 17 \\), which is prime. So, \\( m = 7 * 17 = 119 \\). Then, \\( n = (7 - 1)(17 - 1) = 6 * 16 = 96 \\). The proper divisors of 96 are many: 2,3,4,6,8,12,16,24,32,48. Not just 6 and 16. So, this doesn't work. Hence, this approach fails.\n\nAlternatively, if \\( p - 1 \\) and \\( q - 1 \\) are both powers of 2, then \\( n \\) will be a power of 2 multiplied by another power of 2, i.e., a higher power of 2. But then, \\( n \\) will be a prime power, and its proper divisors will be 2,4,..., up to \\( 2^{k-1} \\). So, for example, if \\( p - 1 = 2 \\), \\( q - 1 = 8 \\), then \\( p = 3 \\), \\( q = 9 \\), which is not prime. Invalid.\n\nWait, but in the case of \\( m = 15 \\), \\( p = 3 \\), \\( q = 5 \\). \\( p - 1 = 2 \\), \\( q - 1 = 4 \\), which are both powers of 2. So, \\( n = 8 \\), which is a prime power, and its proper divisors are 2 and 4. If we can find other primes \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) such that \\( p - 1 \\) and \\( q - 1 \\) are powers of 2, then \\( n \\) would be a power of 2, and its proper divisors would be those powers.\n\nPrimes \\( p \\) such that \\( p - 1 \\) is a power of 2 are known as Fermat primes. Fermat primes are primes of the form \\( 2^{2^k} + 1 \\). Known Fermat primes correspond to \\( k = 0,1,2,3,4 \\): 3,5,17,257,65537.\n\nSo, if we take two Fermat primes, \\( p = 3 \\) and \\( q = 5 \\), then \\( p - 1 = 2 \\), \\( q - 1 = 4 \\), and \\( n