 this with the general formula to ensure consistency.\n\nLet's substitute a = b = c into the general formula.\n\nSet a = b = c, then:\n\ns\u00b2 = [a\u00b2 +a\u00b2 + sqrt(4a\u00b2(a\u00b2 +a\u00b2 -a\u00b2) - (a\u00b2 -a\u00b2)^2)] /2\n\nSimplify inside the square root:\n\n4a\u00b2(a\u00b2 +a\u00b2 -a\u00b2) =4a\u00b2(a\u00b2) =4a^4\n\nAnd (a\u00b2 -a\u00b2)^2 =0\n\nThus, sqrt(4a^4 -0) =sqrt(4a^4)=2a\u00b2\n\nTherefore:\n\ns\u00b2 = [2a\u00b2 +2a\u00b2]/2 = (4a\u00b2)/2 =2a\u00b2\n\nWhich matches our previous result. Good, the general formula holds for the special case.\n\nTherefore, the final answers are:\n\nGeneral case: Area = [a\u00b2 +c\u00b2 + sqrt(4b\u00b2(a\u00b2 +c\u00b2 -b\u00b2) - (a\u00b2 -c\u00b2)^2)] /2\n\nSpecial case when a = b = c: Area =2a\u00b2\n\nBut the problem asks for \"the area of the square\" and \"in the case when a = b = c\". Therefore, the answer should be the expression in terms of a, b, c for the general case, and 2a\u00b2 for the special case.\n\nHowever, the problem might expect a more simplified or factored form. Alternatively, perhaps there is an elegant formula that I\u2019m missing due to the complexity of the general case.\n\nWait, another approach. Let me consider coordinates again. If we set up the square with coordinates as before, and use the three distances to set up equations, maybe we can find a relation that allows us to solve for s without involving x and y.\n\nFrom the three equations:\n\n1. x = (s\u00b2 +a\u00b2 -b\u00b2)/(2s)\n\n2. y = (s\u00b2 +b\u00b2 -c\u00b2)/(2s)\n\n3. x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = a\u00b2\n\nSubstituting x and y into equation 3:\n\n[(s\u00b2 +a\u00b2 -b\u00b2)/(2s)]\u00b2 + [(s\u00b2 +b\u00b2 -c\u00b2)/(2s)]\u00b2 =a\u00b2\n\nMultiply through by 4s\u00b2:\n\n(s\u00b2 +a\u00b2 -b\u00b2)^2 + (s\u00b2 +b\u00b2 -c\u00b2)^2 =4a\u00b2 s\u00b2\n\nLet me bring everything to one side:\n\n(s\u00b2 +a\u00b2 -b\u00b2)^2 + (s\u00b2 +b\u00b2 -c\u00b2)^2 -4a\u00b2 s\u00b2 =0\n\nExpand the squares:\n\nFirst term: s^4 +2s\u00b2(a\u00b2 -b\u00b2) + (a\u00b2 -b\u00b2)^2\n\nSecond term: s^4 +2s\u00b2(b\u00b2 -c\u00b2) + (b\u00b2 -c\u00b2)^2\n\nThird term: -4a\u00b2 s\u00b2\n\nCombine:\n\n2s^4 +2s\u00b2(a\u00b2 -b\u00b2 +b\u00b2 -c\u00b2) + (a\u00b2 -b\u00b2)^2 + (b\u00b2 -c\u00b2)^2 -4a\u00b2 s\u00b2=0\n\nSimplify:\n\n2s^4 +2s\u00b2(a\u00b2 -c\u00b2) + (a\u00b2 -b\u00b2)^2 + (b\u00b2 -c\u00b2)^2 -4a\u00b2 s\u00b2=0\n\nThen:\n\n2s^4 + (2a\u00b2 -2c\u00b2 -4a\u00b2)s\u00b2 + ...=0\n\nWhich is:\n\n2s^4 -2(a\u00b2 +c\u00b2)s\u00b2 + (a\u00b2 -b\u00b2)^2 + (b\u00b2 -c\u00b2)^2=0\n\nSame quartic equation.\n\nTherefore, there's no escaping this quartic equation. Therefore, the area must be as given by the formula above.\n\nTherefore, the answer for the general case is:\n\nArea = [a\u00b2 + c\u00b2 + sqrt{4b\u00b2(a\u00b2 +c\u00b2 -b\u00b2) - (a\u00b2 -c\u00b2)^2}]/2\n\nAnd when a = b = c, Area =2a\u00b2.\n\nBut to present it in a boxed format as per instructions:\n\nThe area of the square in the general case is [ (a\u00b2 + c\u00b2) + sqrt(4b\u00b2(a\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - b\u00b2) - (a\u00b2 - c\u00b2)\u00b2) ] / 2, and in the special case when a = b = c, the area is 2a\u00b2.\n\nBut maybe the problem expects a more simplified expression or perhaps the answer is known in terms of the British Flag Theorem.\n\nWait, another thought. Using the British Flag Theorem, which states PA\u00b2 + PC\u00b2 = PB\u00b2 + PD\u00b2.\n\nWe have PD\u00b2 = a\u00b2 +c\u00b2 -b\u00b2\n\nBut also, since PD is the distance from P to D, which is sqrt(x\u00b2 + (s - y)^2). From earlier expressions:\n\nx = (s\u00b2 +a\u00b2 -b\u00b2)/(2s)\n\ns - y = (s\u00b2 -b\u00b2 +c\u00b2)/(2s)\n\nThus, PD\u00b2 = x\u00b2 + (s - y)^2 = [(s\u00b2 +a\u00b2 -b\u00b2)^2 + (s\u00b2 -b\u00b2 +c\u00b2)^2]/(4s\u00b2)\n\nBut from British Flag Theorem, PD\u00b2 =a\u00b2 +c\u00b2 -b\u00b2\n\nThus,\n\n[(s\u00b2 +a\u00b2 -b\u00b2)^2 + (s\u00b2 -b\u00b2 +c\u00b2)^2]/(4s\u00b2) =a\u00b2 +c\u00b2 -b\u00b2\n\nMultiply both sides by 4s\u00b2:\n\n(s\u