r CD would be a line through the origin at angle \u03b8. The projection of M onto AB is P, which is (r cos \u03c6, 0). The projection of M onto CD is Q. To find Q, we need to find the foot of the perpendicular from M to CD. Let me parametrize CD. Since CD is a diameter, its direction is given by angle \u03b8. So the line CD can be represented as y = tan \u03b8 x. To find the foot of the perpendicular from M (r cos \u03c6, r sin \u03c6) to CD, we can use the formula for projection.\n\nThe formula for the foot of the perpendicular from a point (x0, y0) to the line ax + by + c = 0 is given by:\n\n(x, y) = ( (b(bx0 - ay0) - ac ) / (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ), (a(-bx0 + ay0) - bc ) / (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ) )\n\nBut in our case, line CD is y = tan \u03b8 x, which can be rewritten as tan \u03b8 x - y = 0. So a = tan \u03b8, b = -1, c = 0.\n\nSo the foot Q would be:\n\nx = [ (-1)(- tan \u03b8 * r cos \u03c6 - (-1) * r sin \u03c6 ) - 0 ] / (tan\u00b2 \u03b8 + 1 )\n\nWait, let me compute this step by step. Let me recall the formula correctly.\n\nGiven a line ax + by + c = 0, the projection of (x0, y0) is:\n\nx = x0 - a*(a x0 + b y0 + c)/(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\ny = y0 - b*(a x0 + b y0 + c)/(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\nSo for the line tan \u03b8 x - y = 0 (i.e., a = tan \u03b8, b = -1, c = 0), then:\n\nThe projection Q of M (r cos \u03c6, r sin \u03c6) is:\n\nx = r cos \u03c6 - tan \u03b8*(tan \u03b8 * r cos \u03c6 - 1 * r sin \u03c6 + 0)/(tan\u00b2 \u03b8 + 1)\n\ny = r sin \u03c6 - (-1)*(tan \u03b8 * r cos \u03c6 - 1 * r sin \u03c6 + 0)/(tan\u00b2 \u03b8 + 1)\n\nSimplify the denominator: tan\u00b2 \u03b8 + 1 = sec\u00b2 \u03b8 = 1 / cos\u00b2 \u03b8\n\nSo:\n\nx = r cos \u03c6 - tan \u03b8 [ tan \u03b8 r cos \u03c6 - r sin \u03c6 ] * cos\u00b2 \u03b8\n\nSimilarly,\n\ny = r sin \u03c6 + [ tan \u03b8 r cos \u03c6 - r sin \u03c6 ] * cos\u00b2 \u03b8\n\nLet me compute the term inside the brackets:\n\ntan \u03b8 r cos \u03c6 - r sin \u03c6 = r ( tan \u03b8 cos \u03c6 - sin \u03c6 )\n\nBut tan \u03b8 = sin \u03b8 / cos \u03b8, so:\n\n= r ( (sin \u03b8 / cos \u03b8) cos \u03c6 - sin \u03c6 )\n\n= r ( sin \u03b8 cos \u03c6 / cos \u03b8 - sin \u03c6 )\n\n= r [ (sin \u03b8 cos \u03c6 - sin \u03c6 cos \u03b8 ) / cos \u03b8 ]\n\nUsing the sine subtraction formula: sin(A - B) = sin A cos B - cos A sin B\n\nSo sin \u03b8 cos \u03c6 - sin \u03c6 cos \u03b8 = sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6)\n\nThus,\n\n= r [ sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6) / cos \u03b8 ]\n\nSo the term inside the brackets is r sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6)/cos \u03b8\n\nSo substituting back into x and y:\n\nx = r cos \u03c6 - tan \u03b8 * [ r sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6)/cos \u03b8 ] * cos\u00b2 \u03b8\n\nSimplify tan \u03b8 = sin \u03b8 / cos \u03b8:\n\nx = r cos \u03c6 - (sin \u03b8 / cos \u03b8) * r sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6) / cos \u03b8 * cos\u00b2 \u03b8\n\nSimplify:\n\nThe cos\u00b2 \u03b8 cancels with 1/cos \u03b8 * cos\u00b2 \u03b8 = cos \u03b8:\n\nx = r cos \u03c6 - sin \u03b8 * r sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6)\n\nSimilarly,\n\ny = r sin \u03c6 + [ r sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6)/cos \u03b8 ] * cos\u00b2 \u03b8\n\n= r sin \u03c6 + r sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6) * cos \u03b8\n\nSo x component:\n\nx = r cos \u03c6 - r sin \u03b8 sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6)\n\ny = r sin \u03c6 + r cos \u03b8 sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6)\n\nNow, PQ is the segment from P (r cos \u03c6, 0) to Q (x, y). So the coordinates of Q are (x, y) as above.\n\nSo the distance PQ is sqrt[(x - r cos \u03c6)^2 + (y - 0)^2] = sqrt[( - r sin \u03b8 sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6) )^2 + ( r sin \u03c6 + r cos \u03b8 sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6) )^2 ]\n\nLet me compute each part:\n\nFirst term: (- r sin \u03b8 sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6))^2 = r\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 sin\u00b2(\u03b8 - \u03c6)\n\nSecond term: [ r sin \u03c6 + r cos \u03b8 sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6) ]^2 = r\u00b2 [ sin \u03c6 + cos \u03b8 sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6) ]\u00b2\n\nSo let's expand the second term:\n\nsin \u03c6 + cos \u03b8 sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6)\n\nWe can use the sine subtraction formula on sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6):\n\nsin(\u03b8 - \u03c6) = sin \u03b8 cos \u03c6 - cos \u03b8 sin \u03c6\n\nSo cos \u03b8 sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6) = cos \u03b8 ( sin \u03b8 cos \u03c6 - cos \u03b8 sin \u03c6 ) = sin \u03b8 cos \u03b8 cos \u03c6 - cos\u00b2 \u03b8 sin \u03c6\n\nTherefore,\n\nsin \u03c6 + cos \u03b8 sin(\u03b8 - \u03c6) = sin \u03c6 + sin \u03b8 cos \u03b8 cos \u03c6 - cos\u00b2 \u03b8 sin \u03c6\n\nFactor sin \u03c6:\n\n= sin \u03c6 (1 - cos\u00b2 \u03b8) + sin \u03b8 cos \u03b8 cos \u03c6\n\nBut 1 - cos\u00b2 \u03b8 = sin\u00b2 \u03b8, so:\n\n= sin \u03c6 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 + sin \u03b8 cos \u03b8 cos \u03c6\n\nFactor sin \u03b8:\n\n= sin \u03b8 ( sin \u03c6 sin \u03b8 + cos \u03b8 cos \u03c6 )\n\nNote that sin \u03c6 sin \u03b8 + cos \u03b8 cos \u03c6 = cos(\u03b8 - \u03c6) by the cosine addition formula.\n\nWait, cos(A - B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B. So cos(theta - phi) = cos theta cos phi + sin theta sin phi. But in our case, sin phi sin theta + cos theta cos phi = cos(theta - phi). Therefore:\n\n= sin theta * cos(theta - phi)\n\nTherefore, the second term becomes:\n\nr\u00b2 [ sin theta * cos(theta - phi) ]\u00b2 = r\u00b2 sin\u00b2 theta cos\u00b2(theta - phi)\n\nTherefore, the total PQ squared is:\n\nr\u00b2 sin\u00b2 theta sin\u00b2(theta - phi) + r\u00b2 sin\u00b2 theta cos\u00b2(theta - phi) = r\u00b2 sin\u00b2 theta [ sin\u00b2(theta - phi) + cos\u00b2(theta - phi) ] = r\u00b2 sin\u00b2 theta * 1 = r\u00b2 sin\u00b2 theta\n\nTherefore, PQ = r |sin theta|. Since theta is the angle between diameters AB and CD, which is fixed, PQ is constant, equal to r |sin theta|. Therefore, PQ's length does not depend on phi, i.e., the position of M.\n\nTherefore, regardless of where M is on the circle, PQ's length is r |sin theta|, which is constant. Hence proved.\n\nWait, but in the problem statement, it's not specified that AB and CD are at a fixed angle theta. Wait, but in the problem statement, AB and CD are fixed diameters. So theta is fixed. Therefore, PQ's length is r |sin theta|, which is a constant, independent of M's position.\n\nBut in the problem 