nk. The parametric equations for line BP: starting at B and moving towards P. But since the circle is centered at the origin, maybe I can parametrize the line as:\n\nAny point on BP can be written as B + t(P - B). So, coordinates would be (R cos \u03b8 + t(r - R cos \u03b8), R sin \u03b8 + t(0 - R sin \u03b8)). We need to find the point C which is different from B. Since the line BP intersects the large circle at B and C, substituting into the equation of the large circle x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = R\u00b2. Let me set up the equation.\n\nLet\u2019s denote the parametric coordinates as x = R cos \u03b8 + t(r - R cos \u03b8), y = R sin \u03b8 - t R sin \u03b8.\n\nPlug into x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = R\u00b2:\n\n[R cos \u03b8 + t(r - R cos \u03b8)]\u00b2 + [R sin \u03b8 - t R sin \u03b8]\u00b2 = R\u00b2.\n\nExpand this:\n\nFirst term: [R cos \u03b8 + t(r - R cos \u03b8)]\u00b2 = R\u00b2 cos\u00b2 \u03b8 + 2 t R cos \u03b8 (r - R cos \u03b8) + t\u00b2 (r - R cos \u03b8)\u00b2\n\nSecond term: [R sin \u03b8 - t R sin \u03b8]\u00b2 = R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 - 2 t R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 + t\u00b2 R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8\n\nAdding both terms:\n\nR\u00b2 cos\u00b2 \u03b8 + 2 t R cos \u03b8 (r - R cos \u03b8) + t\u00b2 (r - R cos \u03b8)\u00b2 + R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 - 2 t R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 + t\u00b2 R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 = R\u00b2\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nR\u00b2 (cos\u00b2 \u03b8 + sin\u00b2 \u03b8) + 2 t R [cos \u03b8 (r - R cos \u03b8) - R sin\u00b2 \u03b8] + t\u00b2 [(r - R cos \u03b8)\u00b2 + R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8] = R\u00b2\n\nSince cos\u00b2 \u03b8 + sin\u00b2 \u03b8 = 1, the first term is R\u00b2. Then:\n\nR\u00b2 + 2 t R [r cos \u03b8 - R cos\u00b2 \u03b8 - R sin\u00b2 \u03b8] + t\u00b2 [ (r\u00b2 - 2 r R cos \u03b8 + R\u00b2 cos\u00b2 \u03b8) + R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 ] = R\u00b2\n\nSimplify the coefficients:\n\nFor the linear term: r cos \u03b8 - R (cos\u00b2 \u03b8 + sin\u00b2 \u03b8) = r cos \u03b8 - R\n\nSo the linear term becomes 2 t R (r cos \u03b8 - R