\u00b2 (R - r cos \u03b8 )\u00b2 / D\n\nC \u00b7 A = - R r cos \u03b8\n\nTherefore, sum A \u00b7 B + B \u00b7 C + C \u00b7 A:\n\n= [ r R cos \u03b8 - 2 r\u00b2 R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 / D ] + [ R\u00b2 - 2 R\u00b2 (R - r cos \u03b8 )\u00b2 / D ] + [ - R r cos \u03b8 ]\n\nSimplify:\n\nr R cos \u03b8 - R r cos \u03b8 cancels out.\n\nThen:\n\n-2 r\u00b2 R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 / D + R\u00b2 - 2 R\u00b2 (R - r cos \u03b8 )\u00b2 / D\n\n= R\u00b2 - [ 2 r\u00b2 R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 + 2 R\u00b2 (R - r cos \u03b8 )\u00b2 ] / D\n\nFactor out 2 R\u00b2 / D:\n\n= R\u00b2 - 2 R\u00b2 [ r\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 + (R - r cos \u03b8 )\u00b2 ] / D\n\nCompute the bracket:\n\nr\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 + (R - r cos \u03b8 )\u00b2 = r\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 + R\u00b2 - 2 R r cos \u03b8 + r\u00b2 cos\u00b2 \u03b8 = R\u00b2 - 2 R r cos \u03b8 + r\u00b2 (sin\u00b2 \u03b8 + cos\u00b2 \u03b8 ) = R\u00b2 - 2 R r cos \u03b8 + r\u00b2 = D\n\nTherefore, the sum becomes:\n\nR\u00b2 - 2 R\u00b2 [ D ] / D = R\u00b2 - 2 R\u00b2 = - R\u00b2\n\nTherefore, A \u00b7 B + B \u00b7 C + C \u00b7 A = - R\u00b2\n\nTherefore, going back to AB\u00b2 + BC\u00b2 + CA\u00b2:\n\n= 2(r\u00b2 + 2 R\u00b2 ) - 2( - R\u00b2 ) = 2 r\u00b2 + 4 R\u00b2 + 2 R\u00b2 = 2 r\u00b2 + 6 R\u00b2\n\nTherefore, regardless of \u03b8, the expression AB\u00b2 + BC\u00b2 + CA\u00b2 is constant, equal to 6 R\u00b2 + 2 r\u00b2. Therefore, the set of values is a single value: {6 R\u00b2 + 2 r\u00b2}\n\nWait, but that seems surprising. Is it possible that this expression is constant?\n\nLet me check with a specific case. Suppose \u03b8 = 0. Then point B is (R, 0). Then line BP is from (R,0) to (r,0), which is the x-axis. The other intersection C is diametrically opposite on the larger circle? Wait, no. If we have a line from B (R,0) to P (r,0), extending it, the other intersection C would be on the opposite side of P from B. But since P is inside the larger circle, the line BP starts at B, goes through P, and exits the larger circle again at C. Wait, but in this case, the line BP is along the x-axis from (R,0) to (r,0). If we extend beyond P, we would go outside the smaller circle, but since the larger circle has radius R, the line BP only intersects the large circle at B and C. But when \u03b8 = 0, direction from B to P is towards the center. Wait, but in this case, the line BP is the x-axis from B (R,0) to P (r,0). To find the other intersection point C, we need to parametrize the line beyond P. Wait, but since P is between B and the center, the line BP extended beyond P would leave the large circle at another point C. Wait, but if the line BP is along the x-axis from B (R,0) towards P (r,0), then beyond P, it continues to (-\u221e, 0). But the large circle only extends to x = -R. Therefore, the other intersection point C is (-R, 0). Therefore, when \u03b8 = 0, C is (-R, 0).\n\nThen, the perpendicular to BP at P is the line perpendicular to the x-axis at P (r,0), which is the vertical line x = r. This intersects the smaller circle again at A = (r, r) or (r, -r). But wait, the smaller circle has radius r, so points on x = r are (r, y) with y\u00b2 = r\u00b2 - r\u00b2 = 0. Therefore, only (r,0). But the problem states that if the perpendicular is tangent at P, then A = P. In this case, the vertical line x = r is tangent to the smaller circle at P (r,0), so A = P.\n\nTherefore, when \u03b8 = 0, A = P, B = (R,0), C = (-R,0). Compute AB\u00b2 + BC\u00b2 + CA\u00b2:\n\nAB\u00b2: distance from A=P=(r,0) to B=(R,0): (R - r)^2\n\nBC\u00b2: distance from B=(R,0) to C=(-R,0): (2R)^2 = 4 R\u00b2\n\nCA\u00b2: distance from C=(-R,0) to A=(r,0): (R + r)^2\n\nTherefore, total: (R - r)^2 + 4 R\u00b2 + (R + r)^2 = (R\u00b2 - 2 R r + r\u00b2) + 4 R\u00b2 + (R\u00b2 + 2 R r + r\u00b2) = 6 R\u00b2 + 2 r\u00b2, which matches the formula we derived. So in this case, the value is indeed 6 R\u00b2 + 2 r\u00b2.\n\nSimilarly, take another case, say \u03b8 = \u03c0/2. Then B is (0, R). The line BP connects (0, R) to (r, 0). Let me find point C on the large circle.\n\nParametrize BP: from (0, R) to (r, 0). Parametric equations:\n\nx = t r, y = R - t R, where t ranges from 0 to 1 for BP. To find C, extend beyond P. So t > 1.\n\nSubstitute into x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = R\u00b2:\n\n(t r)^2 + (R - t R)^2 = R\u00b2\n\nt\u00b2 r\u00b2 + R\u00b2 - 2 t R\u00b2 + t\u00b2 R\u00b2 = R\u00b2\n\nt\u00b2 (r\u00b2 + R\u00b2) - 2 t R\u00b2 = 0\n\nt [ t (r\u00b2 + R\u00b2) - 2 R\u00b2 ] = 0\n\nSolutions t=0 (point B) and t = 2 R\u00b2 / (r\u00b2 + R\u00b2)\n\nTherefore, coordinates of C are:\n\nx = (2 R\u00b2 / (r\u00b2 + R\u00b2)) r, y = R - (2 R\u00b2 / (r\u00b2 + R\u00b2)) R = R [ 1 - 2 R\u00b2 / (r\u00b2 + R\u00b2) ] = R [ (r\u00b2 + R\u00b2 - 2 R\u00b2 ) / (r\u00b2 + R\u00b2) ] = R (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2 ) / (r\u00b2 + R\u00b2 )\n\nSo point C is ( 2 R\u00b2 r / (R\u00b2 + r\u00b2 ), R (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2 ) / (R\u00b2 + r\u00b2 ) )\n\nNow, find point A. The perpendicular to BP at P (r,0). The line BP has slope (0 - R)/(r - 0) = - R/r. Therefore, the perpendicular has slope r/R. Equation: y = (r/R)(x - r)\n\nIntersect this with the smaller circle x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = r\u00b2:\n\nx\u00b2 + (r\u00b2/R\u00b2)(x - r)^2 = r\u00b2\n\nMultiply through by R\u00b2:\n\nR\u00b2 x\u00b2 + r\u00b2 (x\u00b2 - 2 r x + r\u00b2 ) = r\u00b2 R\u00b2\n\nExpand:\n\nR\u00b2 x\u00b2 + r\u00b2 x\u00b2 - 2 r\u00b3 x + r\u2074 = r\u00b2 R\u00b2\n\nCombine terms:\n\n(R\u00b2 + r\u00b2) x\u00b2 - 2 r\u00b3 x + r\u2074 - r\u00b2 R\u00b2 = 0\n\nThis quadratic equation. Since we know x = r is a solution (point P), we can factor:\n\n(x - r)[ (R\u00b2 + r\u00b2) x - (r\u00b3 - r R\u00b2 ) ] = 0\n\nCheck: Let me expand (x - r)( (R\u00b2 + r\u00b2 )x - r (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2 ) ):\n\n= (R\u00b2 + r\u00b2 )x\u00b2 - r (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2 )x - r (R\u00b2 + r\u00b2 )x + r\u00b2 (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2 )\n\n= (R\u00b2 + r\u00b2 )x\u00b2 - [ r (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2 ) + r (R\u00b2 + r\u00b2 ) ]x + r\u00b2 (r\u00b2 - R\u00b2 )\n\nSimplify coefficients:\n\nThe 