n ellipse centered at ( R\u00b2 / 2, 0 ) in (X,Y) coordinates, with semi-major axis R\u00b2 / 2 and semi-minor axis R / 2.\n\nReverting back to original variables:\n\nX = x / ( R r ), Y = y / r\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\n[ ( x / ( R r ) - R\u00b2 / 2 )\u00b2 ] / ( R^4 / 4 ) + [ ( y / r )\u00b2 ] / ( R\u00b2 / 4 ) = 1\n\nMultiply out denominators:\n\n4 ( x / ( R r ) - R\u00b2 / 2 )\u00b2 / R^4 + 4 ( y / r )\u00b2 / R\u00b2 = 1\n\nSimplify:\n\n4 ( x - R\u00b3 r / 2 )\u00b2 / ( R^6 r\u00b2 ) + 4 y\u00b2 / ( R\u00b2 r\u00b2 ) = 1\n\nMultiply both sides by R\u00b2 r\u00b2:\n\n4 ( x - R\u00b3 r / 2 )\u00b2 / R^4 + 4 y\u00b2 = R\u00b2 r\u00b2\n\nDivide by 4:\n\n( x - R\u00b3 r / 2 )\u00b2 / R^4 + y\u00b2 = R\u00b2 r\u00b2 / 4\n\nMultiply through by R^4:\n\n( x - R\u00b3 r / 2 )\u00b2 + R^4 y\u00b2 = R^6 r\u00b2 / 4\n\nThis equation seems complicated, but note that in the normalized coordinates, it's an ellipse. Therefore, the locus of M is an ellipse.\n\nAlternatively, in the step where we obtained x\u00b2 - R\u00b2 r x + R\u00b2 y\u00b2 = 0, this can be rewritten as:\n\nx\u00b2 - R\u00b2 r x + (R\u00b2 y\u00b2) = 0\n\nDivide by R\u00b2:\n\n(x\u00b2)/R\u00b2 - r x + y\u00b2 = 0\n\nComplete the square for x:\n\n(x\u00b2)/R\u00b2 - r x = (x/R\u00b2)(x - R\u00b2 r )\n\nHmm, not straightforward. Let me complete the square:\n\nTake x\u00b2/R\u00b2 - r x = (x\u00b2 - R\u00b2 r x R\u00b2)/R\u00b2 = ?\n\nWait, perhaps better to write in standard form.\n\nFrom x\u00b2 - R\u00b2 r x + R\u00b2 y\u00b2 = 0\n\nRewrite as:\n\nx\u00b2 - R\u00b2 r x + (R\u00b2 r / 2 )\u00b2 + R\u00b2 y\u00b2 = (R\u00b2 r / 2 )\u00b2\n\nThen,\n\n(x - R\u00b2 r / 2 )\u00b2 + R\u00b2 y\u00b2 = ( R\u00b2 r / 2 )\u00b2\n\nThis is the equation of an ellipse centered at ( R\u00b2 r / 2, 0 ) with semi-major axis R\u00b2 r / 2 along the x-axis and semi-minor axis R r / 2 along the y-axis.\n\nTherefore, the locus of the midpoint M of BC is an ellipse with center at ( (R\u00b2 r)/2, 0 ), semi-major axis of length (R\u00b2 r)/2, and semi-minor axis of length (R r)/2.\n\nThus, the answer to part (ii) is an ellipse with the equation (x - (R\u00b2 r)/2 )\u00b2 / ( (R\u00b2 r / 2 )\u00b2 ) + y\u00b2 / ( (R r / 2 )\u00b2 ) = 1.\n\nSimplify this equation:\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator:\n\n[ (x - (R\u00b2 r / 2 ))\u00b2 ] / ( R^4 r\u00b2 / 4 ) + [ y\u00b2 ] / ( R\u00b2 r\u00b2 / 4 ) = 1\n\nWhich can be written as:\n\n4 (x - (R\u00b2 r / 2 ))\u00b2 / ( R^4 r\u00b2 ) + 4 y\u00b2 / ( R\u00b2 r\u00b2 ) = 1\n\nThis is the standard form of an ellipse.\n\nAlternatively, writing without denominators:\n\n( x - (R\u00b2 r / 2 ) )\u00b2 / ( ( R\u00b2 r / 2 )\u00b2 ) + y\u00b2 / ( ( R r / 2 )\u00b2 ) = 1\n\nSo, the locus is an ellipse with center at ( R\u00b2 r / 2, 0 ), major axis R\u00b2 r / 2, and minor axis R r / 2.\n\nTherefore, the answer to part (ii) is the ellipse described above.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\n(i) The set of values is the constant \\(\\boxed{6R^2 + 2r^2}\\).\n\n(ii) The locus is the ellipse given by the equation \\(\\boxed{\\left( x - \\frac{R^2 r}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{2y}{Rr} \\right)^2 = 1}\\).", "deepseek_solution": "Given two concentric circles with radii \\(R > r\\) and the same center, we need to determine the set of values of \\(AB^2 + BC^2 + CA^2\\) and the locus of the midpoint of \\(BC\\) where \\(P\\) is a fixed point on the smaller circle, \\(B\\) is a variable point on the larger circle, and \\(C\\) is the other intersection of line \\(BP\\) with the larger circle. The perpendicular to \\(BP\\) at \\(P\\) meets the smaller circle again at \\(A\\).\n\n### Part (i): Set of Values of \\(AB^2 + BC^2 + CA^2\\)\n\n1. **Coordinate Setup and Parametrization**:\n   - Place the center of the circles at the origin.\n   - Fix \\(P\\) at \\((r, 0)\\) on the smaller circle.\n   - Parameterize \\(B\\) as \\((R \\cos \\theta, R \\sin \\theta)\\) on the larger circle.\n\n2. **Finding Point \\(C\\)**:\n   - The line \\(BP\\) intersects the larger circle again at \\(C\\). Using parametric equations and solving for the intersection, we find the coordinates of \\(C\\).\n\n3. **Finding Point \\(A\\)**:\n   - The perpendicular to \\(BP\\) at \\(P\\) intersects the smaller circle again at \\(A\\). Using the perpendicular slope and solving the intersection, we find the coordinates of \\(A\\).\n\n4. **Vector Analysis and Dot Products**:\n   - Using vector dot products and the properties of the circles, we compute \\(A \\cdot B\\), \\(B \\cdot C\\), and \\(C \\cdot A\\).\n   - After simplification, we find that the sum \\(A \\cdot B + B \\cdot C + C \\cdot A = -R^2\\).\n\n5. **Final Calculation**:\n   - Using the formula for \\(AB^2 + BC^2 + CA^2\\) in terms of vector dot products, we find the expression simplifies to a constant value.\n\n**Result**:\n\\[\nAB^2 + BC^2 + CA^2 = \\boxed{6R^2 + 2r^2}\n\\]\n\n### Part (ii): Locus of the Midpoint of \\(BC\\)\n\n1. **Midpoint Coordinates**:\n   - Calculate the coordinates of the midpoint \\(M\\) of \\(BC\\) using the coordinates of \\(B\\) and \\(C\\).\n\n2. **Simplification and Ellipse Equation**:\n   - Express the coordinates of \\(M\\) in terms of \\(\\theta\\) and simplify.\n   - By eliminating \\(\\theta\\) and using trigonometric identities, we derive the equation of the locus.\n\n**Result**:\nThe locus of the midpoint of \\(BC\\) is the ellipse given by:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left( x - \\frac{R^2 r}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{2y}{Rr} \\right)^2 = 1}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n#### (i) Find the set of values of \\(AB^2 + BC^2 + CA^2\\).\n\n1. **Define Points and Let \\( O \\) be the center of the circles:**\n   - Let \\( P \\) be a fixed point on the smaller circle of radius \\( r \\).\n   - Let \\( B \\) be a variable point on the larger circle of radius \\( R \\).\n   - \\( BP \\) intersects the larger circle at \\( C \\).\n   - The perpendicular to \\( BP \\) at \\( P \\) meets the smaller circle again at \\( A \\).\n   - Let \\( M \\) be the midpoint of \\( BC \\) and \\( PM = x \\).\n   - Let \\( O \\) be the common center of both circles.\n\n2. **Angle and Lengths:**\n   - Since \\(\\angle APQ = 90^\\circ\\), \\( AQ \\) is a diameter of the smaller circle.\n   - Therefore, the length of \\( AQ = 2r \\).\n\n3. **Calculate \\( AP^2 \\):**\n   - By the Pythagorean theorem in \\( \\triangle APQ \\):\n     \\[\n     AP^2 = AQ^2 - PQ^2 = (2r)^2 - (2x)^2 = 4r^2 - 4x^2\n     \\]\n\n4. **Calculate \\( BM^2 \\):**\n   - \\( BM \\) is part of the larger circle, and we have:\n     \\[\n     BM^2 = R^2 - OM^2\n     \\]\n   - Since \\( M \\) is the midpoint of \\( BC \\) and assuming \\( Q \\) is also the midpoint of \\( BC \\) due to symmetry:\n     \\[\n     OM^2 = r^2 - x^2\n     \\]\n   - Therefore:\n     \\[\n     BM^2 = R^2 - (r^2 - x^2) = R^2 - r^2 + x^2\n     \\]\n\n5. **Summing the distances:**\n   \\[\n   AB^2 + BC^2 + CA^2 = (AP^2 + (BM - x)^2) + (AP^2 + (BM + x)^2) + BC^2\n   \\]\n\n6. **Expand terms:**\n   - Compute each squared term:\n     \\[\n     BM^2 - 2BMx + x^2 = (R^2 - r^2 + x^2) - 2x \\sqrt{R^2 - r^2 + x^2} + x^2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     BM^2 + 2BMx + x^2 = (R^2 - r^2 + x^2) + 2x \\sqrt{R^2 - r^2 + x^2} + x^2\n     \\]\n   - For \\( BC^2 \\):\n     \\[\n     BC^2 = 4BM^2 = 4(R^2 - r^2 + x^2)\n     \\]\n\n7. **Combine and simplify:**\n   \\[\n   AB^2 + BC^2 + CA^2 = 2 AP^2 + 6BM^2 + 2 x^2\n   \\]\n\n8. **Substitute \\(AP^2\\) and \\(BM^2\\):**\n   \\[\n   AB^2 + BC^2 + CA^2 = 2(4r^2 - 4x^2) + 6(R^2 - r^2 + x^2) + 2x^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   AB^2 + BC^2 + CA^2 = 8r^2 - 8x^2 + 6R^2 - 6r^2 + 6x^2 + 2x^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   AB^2 + BC^2 + CA^2 = 2r^2 + 6R^2\n   \\]\n\n9. **Conclusion for Part (i):**\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{AB^2 + BC^2 + CA^2 = 6R^2 + 2r^2}\n   \\]\n\n#### (ii) Find the locus of the midpoint of \\( BC \\).\n\n1. **Midpoint \\( M \\) relation:**\n   - \\( M \\) is the midpoint of \\( BC \\). As \\( BC \\) crosses the circles at their respective given points \\( B \\) and \\( C \\), \\( M \\) also becomes, symmetrically, the midpoint of PQ.\n\n2. **Transform and shape:**\n   - When shrinking the smaller circle by a factor of 2 with \\( P \\) as the center, \\( Q \\) moves to \\( M \\).\n\n3. **Locus characterization:**\n   - Thus, the locus of \\( M \\) must be the circle obtained by shrinking the given circle around \\( P \\) by a factor of 2.\n\n4. **Conclusion for Part (ii):**\n   - The locus of \\( M \\) is a circle with diameter \\( OP \\):\n     \\[\n     \\boxed{\\text{The locus of \\( M \\) is the circle with diameter equal to OP}}\n     \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In a kingdom of animals, tigers always tell the truth, foxes always lie, and monkeys sometimes tell the truth and sometimes lie. There are 100 animals of each kind, divided into 100 groups, with each group containing exactly 2 animals of one kind and 1 animal of another kind. After grouping, Kung Fu Panda asked each animal in each group, \"Is there a tiger in your group?\" and 138 animals responded \"yes.\" He then asked, \"Is there a fox in your group?\" and 188 animals responded \"yes.\" How many monkeys told the truth both times?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's tackle this problem step by step. First, let me make sure I understand the problem correctly. We have a kingdom with tigers, foxes, and monkeys, each species having 100 individuals. These 300 animals are divided into 100 groups, each group containing exactly 2 animals of one kind and 1 animal of another kind. So each group has 3 animals: 2 of one species and 1 of another. \n\nThen Kung Fu Panda asks each animal two questions: first, \"Is there a tiger in your group?\" and 138 animals answered \"yes.\" Then he asks, \"Is there a fox in your group?\" with 188 responding \"yes.\" The question is how many monkeys told the truth both times.\n\nAlright, so we need to figure out how many monkeys answered both questions truthfully. Let's break this down. \n\nFirst, let's consider the composition of the groups. Each group has 3 animals: 2 of one kind and 1 of another. So possible group types are:\n\n1. 2 tigers + 1 fox\n2. 2 tigers + 1 monkey\n3. 2 foxes + 1 tiger\n4. 2 foxes + 1 monkey\n5. 2 monkeys + 1 tiger\n6. 2 monkeys + 1 fox\n\nWait, but the problem says \"each group containing exactly 2 animals of one kind and 1 animal of another kind.\" So each group must be 2 of one species and 1 of another. So all groups are either 2A + 1B, where A and B are different species. So there are 6 possible types as listed above.\n\nBut since there are 100 groups, and 100 of each species, we need to make sure that the total number of each species across all groups adds up to 100. So let's denote variables for the number of each group type. Let's say:\n\n- Let T2F1 be the number of groups with 2 tigers and 1 fox.\n- T2M1 be the number of groups with 2 tigers and 1 monkey.\n- F2T1 be the number of groups with 2 foxes and 1 tiger.\n- F2M1 be the number of groups with 2 foxes and 1 monkey.\n- M2T1 be the number of groups with 2 monkeys and 1 tiger.\n- M2F1 be the number of groups with 2 monkeys and 1 fox.\n\nThen, the total number of tigers would be:\n\n2*T2F1 + 2*T2M1 + 1*F2T1 + 1*M2T1 = 100\n\nSimilarly for foxes:\n\n1*T2F1 + 2*F2T1 + 2*F2M1 + 1*M2F1 = 100\n\nAnd for monkeys:\n\n1*T2M1 + 1*F2M1 + 2*M2T1 + 2*M2F1 = 100\n\nBut that might be a bit complex. Maybe there's a smarter way. Let's think about each group and how the animals would answer the questions.\n\nFirst question: \"Is there a tiger in your group?\"\n\nTigers always tell the truth. So if a tiger is in a group, since there must be tigers in the group (as the tiger is there), the tiger will answer \"yes.\" If a tiger is not in the group, but wait, tigers are in groups where they are either part of the 2 or the 1. Wait, no, a tiger is in a group either as 2 tigers +1 other or 1 tiger + 2 others. Wait, no, each group is 2 of one kind and 1 of another. So a tiger can be in a group either as part of the pair (2 tigers +1 other) or as the singleton (1 tiger + 2 others). \n\nWait, if a tiger is in a group, regardless of whether they are in the pair or the singleton, the group does contain a tiger. So any tiger, when asked \"Is there a tiger in your group?\" will truthfully answer \"yes.\"\n\nFoxes always lie. So if a fox is asked \"Is there a tiger in your group?\", the fox will lie. So if the group actually has a tiger, the fox would say \"no.\" If the group does not have a tiger, the fox would say \"yes.\" Wait, but groups can only have two species: either 2 of one and 1 of another. So a group that has foxes must have either 2 foxes +1 other or 1 fox +2 others. Wait, but since each group is 2 of one kind and 1 of another, a group with foxes must be either 2 foxes +1 other or 1 fox +2 others. So a fox is in a group. Let's think: if a fox is in a group that has a tiger, then the fox is either in a group with 2 foxes +1 tiger or 1 fox +2 tigers. Wait, no: groups are 2 of one kind and 1 of another. So if a group has foxes and tigers, it's either 2 foxes +1 tiger or 2 tigers +1 fox. Similarly for other combinations.\n\nTherefore, a fox in a group with tigers (either 2 foxes +1 tiger or 2 tigers +1 fox) would be asked \"Is there a tiger in your group?\" Since there is a tiger, the truthful answer is \"yes,\" but since the fox lies, it will say \"no.\" Conversely, if a fox is in a group without tigers, then the group must be 2 foxes +1 monkey or 2 monkeys +1 fox. In such cases, the truthful answer to \"Is there a tiger in your group?\" is \"no,\" so the fox lies and says \"yes.\"\n\nMonkeys can either lie or tell the truth. So their answers are variable. We need to model their behavior based on the group composition and whether they choose to tell the truth or lie each time.\n\nSimilarly, for the second question, \"Is there a fox in your group?\", tigers will answer truthfully, foxes will lie, and monkeys can go either way.\n\nWe need to connect the number of \