e arrives even earlier than his usual time).\n\n2. **Define variables**:\n   - Let \\( T \\) be the usual travel time.\n   - Let \\( v \\) be the usual speed.\n   - Let \\( D \\) be the distance to work.\n   \n   Using the fact that \\( v = \\frac{D}{T} \\).\n\n3. **Calculate increased speed**: \n   - His speed with the 60% increase is \\( 1.6v \\).\n\n4. **Determine the new travel time**: \n   - When he departs 40 minutes late, he increases his speed to \\( 1.6v \\) and travels for \\( T - 40 \\) minutes.\n\n5. **Set up the equation**:\n   - The new time taken to cover the same distance \\( D \\) at the increased speed is calculated as:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{D}{1.6v}\n   \\]\n   \n   Since he arrives at 8:35 AM:\n   - His total time savings are \\( 40 \\) minutes (departure delay) + \\( 25 \\) minutes (early arrival): \\( 65 \\) minutes\n   - Hence, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{T}{1.6} = T - 65\n   \\]\n\n6. **Solve for \\( T \\)**:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{T}{1.6} = T - 65\n   \\]\n   Multiplying both sides by 1.6:\n   \\[\n   T = 1.6T - 104\n   \\]\n   Rearrange terms:\n   \\[\n   104 = 0.6T\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( T \\):\n   \\[\n   T = \\frac{104}{0.6} = \\frac{520}{3} \\approx 173.33 \\text{ minutes}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Calculate adjusted time**:\n   - The new time needed to travel \\( D \\) at a modified speed (to reach at 9:00 AM when 40 minutes late):\n   \\[\n   T - 40 = \\frac{520}{3} - 40 = \\frac{520}{3} - \\frac{120}{3} = \\frac{400}{3}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Determine the necessary speed increase**:\n   - To cover \\( D \\) in \\(\\frac{400}{3}\\) minutes instead of \\(\\frac{520}{3}\\) minutes:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Required new speed} = v \\times \\frac{T}{T - 40} = v \\times \\frac{\\frac{520}{3}}{\\frac{400}{3}} = v \\times 1.3\n   \\]\n   - Hence, his speed needs to be increased by 30% to arrive on time at 9:00 AM.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{30 \\%}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given 