e compound inequality into two separate inequalities and solve each part individually. That usually helps with these kinds of problems.\n\nStarting with the left part of the inequality: \\(a < 9a^3 - 11a\\). Let me rearrange this to bring all terms to one side. Subtract \\(a\\) from both sides:\n\n\\(0 < 9a^3 - 11a - a\\)\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\n\\(0 < 9a^3 - 12a\\)\n\nSo that simplifies to:\n\n\\(9a^3 - 12a > 0\\)\n\nI can factor out a common term here. Both terms have a factor of \\(3a\\), right?\n\n\\(3a(3a^2 - 4) > 0\\)\n\nSo, the inequality is \\(3a(3a^2 - 4) > 0\\). Let me write that as:\n\n\\(3a(3a^2 - 4) > 0\\)\n\nTo solve this inequality, I need to find the critical points where the expression equals zero. Setting each factor equal to zero:\n\n1. \\(3a = 0\\) \u21d2 \\(a = 0\\)\n2. \\(3a^2 - 4 = 0\\) \u21d2 \\(a^2 = \\(\\frac{4}{3}\\)\\) \u21d2 \\(a = \\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\) \u2248 \u00b11.1547\n\nSo the critical points are at \\(a = -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\), \\(0\\), and \\(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\). These divide the real number line into four intervals. I need to test each interval to see where the product is positive.\n\nThe intervals are:\n\n1. \\(a < -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\)\n2. \\(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} < a < 0\\)\n3. \\(0 < a < \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\)\n4. \\(a > \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\)\n\nLet's pick test points in each interval.\n\n1. For \\(a < -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\), let's take \\(a = -2\\):\n   \\(3*(-2)(3*(-2)^2 - 4) = 3*(-2)(12 - 4) = -6*8 = -48 < 0\\). So negative here.\n\n2. For \\(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} < a < 0\\), let's take \\(a = -1\\):\n   \\(3*(-1)(3*(-1)^2 - 4) = -3*(3 - 4) = -3*(-1) = 3 > 0\\). Positive here.\n\n3. For \\(0 < a < \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\), let's take \\(a = 1\\):\n   \\(3*1*(3*1^2 - 4) = 3*(3 - 4) = 3*(-1) = -3 < 0\\). Negative here.\n\n4. For \\(a > \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\), let's take \\(a = 2\\):\n   \\(3*2*(3*4 - 4) = 6*(12 - 4) = 6*8 = 48 > 0\\). Positive here.\n\nSo the inequality \\(3a(3a^2 - 4) > 0\