rime. So 499 and 500 are coprime. Therefore, this works. So the three fractions are 1/1000, 1/1000, 499/500.\n\nNow, Petya's sum is (1 + 1 + 499)/(1000 + 1000 + 500) = 501/2500 = 0.2004, which is still more than 1/10. So not good.\n\nBut what if we take more extreme denominators? Let's try with denominators 10000. So two fractions are 1/10000 each. Their sum is 2/10000 = 1/5000. Then the third fraction is 4999/5000. Then, Petya's sum is (1 + 1 + 4999)/(10000 + 10000 + 5000) = 5001/25000 = 0.20004, which is still 20%. Hmm. Same ratio. Wait, why is that?\n\nBecause if we take two fractions as 1/n and 1/n, then the third fraction is (1 - 2/n). So numerator of third fraction is (n - 2), denominator is n. Then, Petya's sum is (1 + 1 + (n - 2))/(n + n + n) = (n)/3n = 1/3. Wait, but that can't be. Wait, hold on. Wait, denominators are n, n, n. But in reality, the third fraction's denominator isn't n. Wait, no. Wait, if the first two fractions are 1/n, their denominators are n. The third fraction is (1 - 2/n), which is (n - 2)/n. So the third fraction is (n - 2)/n, denominator n. So all three denominators are n. So sum of denominators is 3n. Sum of numerators is 1 + 1 + (n - 2) = n. So Petya's sum is n/(3n) = 1/3. So regardless of n, Petya's sum is 1/3. That's strange. Wait, but in my previous examples, the third fraction had a different denominator. Oh, right. If the third fraction's denominator is not n. Wait, maybe I confused.\n\nWait, in my earlier example, I had two fractions with denominator 1000 and the third with denominator 500. That's different. So let's correct that.\n\nIf we take two fractions with denominator n, say 1/n and 1/n, then their sum is 2/n. So the third fraction must be (1 - 2/n). But to write this as a fraction, we need to have a denominator. Let's say the third fraction is (n - 2)/n. So denominator is n. Then all three fractions have denominator n, so adding them gives 1/n + 1/n + (n - 2)/n = (1 + 1 + n - 2)/n = n/n = 1. So that works. Then Petya's